> that called different programs depending on the
> result of a computation performed in BASIC, etc.

(I think the thesis in this paragraph could be disputed
and discussed very controversially; I'll abstain to do
that here in RGRN. Even if marked [OT] it would lead too
far.)

> 
> Actually, most of the stuff that I used to do with
> batch files, I wouldn't try to do in a shell script,
> on a modern system, because sh is too primitive.

Interesting view. Calling the standard Unix shell primitiv
in comparison to the MS bat interpreter. - I suppose this
makes sense only if we argue that the current version of
the bat interpreter has so many ad hoc features stuffed in
that you find one or two features not available in shell.
The point of shell is of course to not built all into it;
rather to have a powerful and consistent framework that
can also make use external utilities in well defined and
powerful ways. And even the features that not yet made it
into the official standard are often supported by various
POSIX compliant shells (bash, ksh, zsh).[*]

> I mean, it *could* do those things, and people used
> to *have* to do those things in sh, which was no
> doubt easier than when I did them in batch files;
> but it's not the easiest way now.  I have better
> options these days.  I could use Perl, for example.
> Why would I write eighty lines of hairy sh when 
> I can write eight lines of clear, maintainable Perl?

It's possible (and probably even easily possible) to write
"hairy" code in shell. Compared to bat syntax, though, I'd
say that it's impossible to write non-trivial readable and
maintainable code in bat; it's just too primitive and
incoherent.

We can certainly agree that it makes always sense to choose
a more appropriate tool depending on the circumstances and
actual task. But it would never appear to me as sensible to
take MS bat files into consideration for any serious tasks.

> 
> You know, I really like Unix -- I certainly prefer it
> to Windows -- but calling the Unix shell "standardised"
> is a bit like calling Windows 95 "stable".  It's if
> anything less standardized than C.

You may not know, but the fact is it is standardised.[**]

Janis

